ul.\n- Play T1: Not useful.\n- Play J1: Single dragon. Discarding it might be okay.\n- Play W4: I have two. Discarding one might allow keeping the chow W2-W3-W4. If I discard one W4, I can still have the chow if I get another W4? Wait, no. A chow is three consecutive, so if I have W2, W3, W4, that's a chow. If I have two W4s, I can use one for the chow and the other for a pair or another meld. But if I discard one W4, I still have one left. So maybe keeping W2, W3, and one W4 is better for a chow.\n\nSo, if I discard one W4, I can keep the chow possibility. Let's see. The unshown tiles have four W4s. So maybe another player has some, but I can still form the chow. So discarding W4 might be a good move. \n\nBut what's the current state of my hand? If I discard W4, then I have W2, W3, W4 (one left). That's three, which can form a chow. So that's a possible meld. If I keep that, then maybe I can later form a chow and get points from it. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard a tile that's not part of any potential meld, like T1 or T4 or B2, that might be better for concealed hand. But if I discard those, I can still keep the W2-W3-W4 chow. \n\nWait, if I discard T1, which is a terminal, maybe I can focus on other patterns. But I'm not sure. \n\nAnother angle: since I have a pair of F1s, maybe I can aim for a hand with multiple pairs or pungs. If I can get another F1, that would form a pung, which is good. But there are three F1s left. \n\nBut in the game history, the previous player played F2, and the next player played W6 and W1. The player across played W6 and J1. So maybe other players have some of those tiles. \n\nIf I discard F1, I might be giving someone the chance to form a pung. Since I have two F1s, if I discard one, another player could Peng if they have one. But if I keep both, I can use them for a pair or pung later. \n\nSo, considering all this, the best move might be to discard a tile that's not part of any potential meld. For example, T1, T4, B2. Let's see whic